\details{
The Binomial distribution with \code{size} \eqn{= n} and \code{prob} \eqn{= p} has
probability mass function:
\deqn{p(x) = \binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \quad x = 0, \ldots, n}
and zero elsewhere.

Invalid arguments give \code{NaN}, as in base R, but without a warning (that is
\code{prob} outside \eqn{[0, 1]}, or a \code{size} that is negative or not a whole
number). \code{size} is taken as a whole number with the same tolerance as \code{x}
(see Non-Integer Values).

\code{nv_dbinom} uses the saddle point expansion of
Loader (2000), as base R does. \code{nv_pbinom} uses the
incomplete beta function methods of DiDonato & Morris (1992)
that base R's \code{\link[stats:pbeta]{stats::pbeta()}} uses: a continued fraction, and an asymptotic
expansion for large \code{size} near the mean. If the continued fraction has not
converged after 1000 iterations, the result is \code{NaN}. \code{nv_qbinom} finds the
smallest \eqn{x} with \eqn{P(X \le x) \ge p}, starting from a Cornish-Fisher
approximation as base R does. Above \code{2^24} in \code{f32} and \code{2^53} in \code{f64} not
every whole number is a float, and \code{nv_qbinom} then returns the smallest float
\eqn{x} with \eqn{P(X \le x) \ge p}.
}
\section{Non-Integer Values}{

\code{x} and \code{q} are counts, but any value is accepted, and one that is not a
whole number is treated as in base R, without its warnings:
\itemize{
\item \code{nv_dbinom()} takes an \code{x} within a relative \code{1e-9} of a whole
number, that is with
\eqn{|x - \mathrm{round}(x)| \le 10^{-9} \max(|x|, 1)}{|x - round(x)| <= 1e-9 * max(|x|, 1)},
as that whole number. Any other \code{x} has probability zero.
\item \code{nv_pbinom()} rounds \code{q} down, except that a \code{q} within \code{1e-7} below
a whole number counts as that number.
\item A negative \code{x} or \code{q} is below the support even where it is within the
tolerance of zero: such an \code{x} has probability zero, and such a \code{q} has
\eqn{P(X \le q) = 0}.
}

Finite quantiles from \code{nv_qbinom()} are always whole numbers. Since
\code{x} and \code{q} are used only as whole numbers, their gradient is zero wherever
a gradient can be taken.
}

\section{Gradients}{

\code{nv_dbinom} can be differentiated with respect to \code{prob}, including at
\code{prob} of 0 and 1. Where the density is zero there, as at \code{prob = 0} for
\code{x > 0}, the log-density is \code{-Inf} and has no derivative: it diverges. With
\code{log = TRUE} the gradient returned there is zero, which is a convention of
this implementation, not the mathematical derivative.
\code{nv_pbinom} iterates until convergence in a \code{\link[=nv_while]{nv_while()}} loop, so
\code{\link[=gradient]{gradient()}} cannot differentiate it. \code{nv_qbinom} is a step function, and
autodiff returns a zero gradient for all of its arguments.
}

\section{Data Types}{

\code{nv_dbinom()}, \code{nv_pbinom()} and \code{nv_qbinom()} compute at
a float data type and return it. If \code{x}/\code{q}/\code{p} is a float, they compute at
its data type; an R double settles on the \link[=default_dtypes]{default float}.
An integer \code{x}/\code{q}/\code{p} (an array or an R integer) is converted to the widest
float among the arrays given for \code{size} or \code{prob}, or to the default float if
there is none. Integers beyond \eqn{2^{24}} are not all exact at \code{f32}, so a
large integer may change in that conversion.

An R value for \code{size} or \code{prob} takes the computing data type. An array is
promoted to it, so a wider one (e.g. \code{f64} for an \code{f32} \code{x}) is an error.
}

